{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You shall not curse the deaf.</b> Scripture mentioned the deaf in order to learn a fortiori: if the deaf, who does not hear when one curses him — the Torah warned concerning him — needless to say one who hears and becomes angered and shamed. And all this is so that a person be guarded in his speech and not accustom his soul to a bad trait; and the prohibition is not on account of his fellow who hears but on account of himself. And if he is guarded in this matter with the deaf, all the more so with one who hears — and therefore Scripture mentioned the deaf, and it serves as a paradigm for all the rest. And further, because Scripture speaks of the common case, that people curse the deaf and put a stumbling block before the blind and do not fear them — and concerning this it says ״ויראת מאלהיך״ (and you shall fear your Elohim), for any matter that is delivered to the heart, it is said of it ״ויראת מאלהיך״. And thus Shlomo, peace be upon him, said (Mishlei 24:12): ״כי תאמר הן לא ידענו זה הלא תוכן לבות הוא יבין ונוצר נפשך הוא ידע והשיב לאדם כפעלו״ — and this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, said: know what is above you — an eye that sees, an ear that hears, and all your deeds are written in a book."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:56.271861232Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}